> table quaternion
multiplication table of the quaternions (row times column), not symmetric: the product does not commute:
      |   1   i   j   k
  ----+----------------
    1 |   1   i   j   k
    i |   i  -1   k  -j
    j |   j  -k  -1   i
    k |   k   j  -i  -1
  i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = ijk = -1 (Hamilton); ij = k, jk = i, ki = j, and reversing a product changes its sign
> table octonion
multiplication table of the octonions (row times column), not symmetric: the product does not commute:
       |    1   e1   e2   e3   e4   e5   e6   e7
  -----+----------------------------------------
     1 |    1   e1   e2   e3   e4   e5   e6   e7
    e1 |   e1   -1   e3  -e2   e5  -e4  -e7   e6
    e2 |   e2  -e3   -1   e1   e6   e7  -e4  -e5
    e3 |   e3   e2  -e1   -1   e7  -e6   e5  -e4
    e4 |   e4  -e5  -e6  -e7   -1   e1   e2   e3
    e5 |   e5   e4  -e7   e6  -e1   -1  -e3   e2
    e6 |   e6   e7   e4  -e5  -e2   e3   -1  -e1
    e7 |   e7  -e6   e5   e4  -e3  -e2   e1   -1
  the Fano plane: the 7 lines (a, b, c) with e_a e_b = e_c, e_b e_c = e_a, e_c e_a = e_b:
    (1, 2, 3) (1, 4, 5) (1, 7, 6) (2, 4, 6) (2, 5, 7) (3, 4, 7) (3, 6, 5)
  every pair of units lies on one line; e_a^2 = -1 and e_a e_b = -e_b e_a for a != b
> algebra quaternion
the quaternions: dimension 4 over the reals, basis 1, i, j, k
  commutative: no: i j = k but j i = -k
  associative: yes ((ab)c = a(bc) at 200 random triples); by Frobenius, R, C and H are the only associative real division algebras
  alternative: yes ((aa)b = a(ab), (ba)a = b(aa) at 200 random pairs)
  flexible and power-associative: yes ((ab)a = a(ba); powers a^n are well defined), as in every Cayley-Dickson algebra
  normed (a composition algebra): yes, |ab| = |a||b| at 200 random pairs (Euler's four-square identity)
  a division algebra: yes, ab = 0 only when a = 0 or b = 0 (it is normed); by Hurwitz, R, C, H and O are the only normed division algebras
  inverses: every a != 0 has a^-1 = conj(a)/|a|^2 (a a^-1 = 1, checked)
  conjugation: conj(ab) = conj(b) conj(a) (checked); a conj(a) = |a|^2
> algebra octonion
the octonions: dimension 8 over the reals, basis 1, e1, e2, e3, e4, e5, e6, e7
  commutative: no: e1 e2 = e3 but e2 e1 = -e3
  associative: no: (e1 e2) e4 = e7 but e1 (e2 e4) = -e7
  alternative: yes ((aa)b = a(ab), (ba)a = b(aa) at 200 random pairs); by Artin's theorem any two elements generate an associative subalgebra
  flexible and power-associative: yes ((ab)a = a(ba); powers a^n are well defined), as in every Cayley-Dickson algebra
  normed (a composition algebra): yes, |ab| = |a||b| at 200 random pairs (Degen's eight-square identity)
  a division algebra: yes, ab = 0 only when a = 0 or b = 0 (it is normed); by Hurwitz, R, C, H and O are the only normed division algebras
  inverses: every a != 0 has a^-1 = conj(a)/|a|^2 (a a^-1 = 1, checked); a^-1(ab) = b holds (alternativity)
  conjugation: conj(ab) = conj(b) conj(a) (checked); a conj(a) = |a|^2
> algebra sedenion
the sedenions: dimension 16 over the reals, basis 1, e1, e2, e3, e4, e5, e6, e7, ...
  commutative: no: e1 e2 = e3 but e2 e1 = -e3
  associative: no: (e1 e2) e4 = e7 but e1 (e2 e4) = -e7
  alternative: no: (aa)b != a(ab) for random a, b (by 7.28)
  flexible and power-associative: yes ((ab)a = a(ba); powers a^n are well defined), as in every Cayley-Dickson algebra
  normed: no: |ab| != |a||b| (off by 0.595 at a random pair)
  a division algebra: no, it has zero divisors: (e1 - e10)(e4 + e15) = 0
  inverses: every a != 0 has a^-1 = conj(a)/|a|^2 (a a^-1 = 1, checked); but a^-1(ab) = b can fail, so ax = b need not be solvable
  conjugation: conj(ab) = conj(b) conj(a) (checked); a conj(a) = |a|^2
> zerodivisors sedenion
zero divisors in the sedenions:
  (e1 - e10)(e4 + e15) = 0, although |e1 - e10| = |e4 + e15| = sqrt(2)
  so |ab| = |a||b| fails and the sedenions are not a division algebra (from dimension 16 on, every Cayley-Dickson algebra has them)
> associator e1, e2, e4
[a, b, c] = (ab)c - a(bc) = e7 - (-e7) = 2 e7
  not associative: the grouping changes the product (in the octonions)
> associator i, j, k
[a, b, c] = (ab)c - a(bc) = -1 - (-1) = 0
  the product of these three is associative (in the quaternions)
> commutator i, j
[a, b] = ab - ba = k - (-k) = 2k
  they do not commute
> rotate (1, 0, 0) by 90 degrees about the z axis
rotate (1, 0, 0) by pi/2 about (0, 0, 1):
  q = cos(theta/2) + sin(theta/2) u, u the unit axis
  q = sqrt(2)/2 + k*sqrt(2)/2: a rotation by pi/2 about (0, 0, 1)
  v = i (the vector as a pure quaternion)
  q v q^-1 = j
  rotated: (0, 1, 0)
  check: |v'| = |v| = 1, the real part is 0, and the rotation matrix gives the same vector
  rotation matrix:
    [0, -1, 0]
    [1, 0, 0]
    [0, 0, 1]
  (right-handed: counterclockwise looking down the axis towards the origin; -q gives the same rotation)
> rotate (1, 2, 3) by 1 + i + j + k
rotate (1, 2, 3) by the quaternion 1 + i + j + k:
  note: q is not a unit quaternion (|q| = 2): normalised
  q = 1/2 + i/2 + j/2 + k/2: a rotation by 2pi/3 about (sqrt(3)/3, sqrt(3)/3, sqrt(3)/3)
  v = i + 2j + 3k (the vector as a pure quaternion)
  q v q^-1 = 3i + j + 2k
  rotated: (3, 1, 2)
  check: |v'| = |v| = sqrt(14), the real part is 0, and the rotation matrix gives the same vector
  rotation matrix:
    [0, 0, 1]
    [1, 0, 0]
    [0, 1, 0]
  (right-handed: counterclockwise looking down the axis towards the origin; -q gives the same rotation)
> rotate (1, 0, 0) by pi/2 about (1, 1, 1)
rotate (1, 0, 0) by pi/2 about (1, 1, 1):
  q = cos(theta/2) + sin(theta/2) u, u the unit axis
  q = sqrt(2)/2 + i*sqrt(6)/6 + j*sqrt(6)/6 + k*sqrt(6)/6: a rotation by pi/2 about (sqrt(3)/3, sqrt(3)/3, sqrt(3)/3)
  v = i (the vector as a pure quaternion)
  q v q^-1 = i/3 + j*(1 + sqrt(3))/3 + k*(1 - sqrt(3))/3
  rotated: (1/3, (1 + sqrt(3))/3, (1 - sqrt(3))/3)
  check: |v'| = |v| = 1, the real part is 0, and the rotation matrix gives the same vector
  rotation matrix:
    [1/3, (1 - sqrt(3))/3, (1 + sqrt(3))/3]
    [(1 + sqrt(3))/3, 1/3, (1 - sqrt(3))/3]
    [(1 - sqrt(3))/3, (1 + sqrt(3))/3, 1/3]
  (right-handed: counterclockwise looking down the axis towards the origin; -q gives the same rotation)
> axisangle (0, 0, 1), 90 degrees
the rotation by pi/2 about (0, 0, 1):
  q = cos(theta/2) + sin(theta/2) u = sqrt(2)/2 + k*sqrt(2)/2
  rotation matrix:
    [0, -1, 0]
    [1, 0, 0]
    [0, 0, 1]
> axisangle 1 + i
  note: q is not a unit quaternion (|q| = sqrt(2)): normalised
sqrt(2)/2 + i*sqrt(2)/2 = cos(theta/2) + sin(theta/2) u:
  theta = pi/2 (90 degrees), axis u = (1, 0, 0)
> rotmatrix 1 + i + j + k
  note: q is not a unit quaternion (|q| = 2): normalised
the rotation matrix of 1/2 + i/2 + j/2 + k/2 (v -> q v q^-1):
  [0, 0, 1]
  [1, 0, 0]
  [0, 1, 0]
> frommatrix 0, -1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1
the unit quaternion of the rotation matrix: q = sqrt(2)/2 + k*sqrt(2)/2 (or -q)
  check: q's matrix equals the given one (max difference 2e-16)
> slerp 1, k, 1/2
slerp(1, k, 1/2) = sqrt(2)/2 + k*sqrt(2)/2
  the arc between them on the unit sphere: a = pi/2 (a rotation by pi); slerp = (sin((1-t)a) q1 + sin(ta) q2)/sin(a)
  check: |slerp| = 1, and q1 (q1^-1 q2)^t = sqrt(2)/2 + k*sqrt(2)/2: the same
> multiply (1+2i) by (3-i)
  ucas: ((1+2i))*((3-i))
5 + 5i
> what is (1+2i)*(3-i)?
  ucas: (1 + 2*i)*(3 - i)
5 + 5i
> what is e to the i pi
  ucas: (e)^(i pi)
-1
> the square root of -4
  ucas: sqrt(-4)
  note: sqrt(-4) is undefined in the reals; its complex principal value follows
  note: on the branch cut of sqrt: the principal value, sqrt z = e^(log(z)/2): i sqrt|z| on the negative reals (the other root is its negative)
2i
> roots of z^3 = 1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
zeros of z^3 - 1 (z complex):
  3 roots (a polynomial of degree 3):
    z = -1/2 - i*sqrt(3)/2
    z = -1/2 + i*sqrt(3)/2
    z = 1
  check: |z^3 - 1| <= 2e-16 at the roots; the polynomial's coefficients come from 64 samples and were checked at 8 other points
  a polynomial of degree 3 has 3 complex roots counted with multiplicity (the fundamental theorem of algebra)
> is z^2 holomorphic
  ucas: holomorphic z^2
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  entire: holomorphic on the whole plane
> is conj(z) analytic?
  ucas: holomorphic conj(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
conj(z) is not holomorphic
  Cauchy-Riemann fails: at z = 1/2 + i/4, df/dx = 1 but df/(i dy) = -1
  (it uses conj, which is not holomorphic)
  Wirtinger derivatives there: df/dz = 0, df/dconj(z) = 1 (holomorphic means df/dconj(z) = 0)
> the residue of 1/(z^2+1) at i
  ucas: residue 1/(z^2+1) at i
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/(z^2 + 1) at z = i:
  = -i/2
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/(z^2 + 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> the poles of 1/(z^2+1)
  ucas: poles 1/(z^2+1)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
poles of 1/(z^2 + 1):
    z = -i  order 1, residue i/2
    z = i  order 1, residue -i/2
  numerically: zeros of 1/f by Newton's method from a grid in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7; orders from the growth of |f| on circles of radius 1e-2..1e-4, residues by circle integrals
> integrate exp(z)/z around the unit circle
  ucas: integrate exp(z)/z around |z| = 1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
integral of exp(z)/z around |z| = 1 (counterclockwise):
  = 2pi*i
  numerically: the trapezoid rule on the circle with 1024 and 2048 points (difference 1e-15)
  residue theorem: 2 pi i times the sum of the residues at the 1 pole inside = 2pi*i: agrees
> rotate (1, 0, 0) by 90 degrees about the z axis using quaternions
rotate (1, 0, 0) by pi/2 about (0, 0, 1):
  q = cos(theta/2) + sin(theta/2) u, u the unit axis
  q = sqrt(2)/2 + k*sqrt(2)/2: a rotation by pi/2 about (0, 0, 1)
  v = i (the vector as a pure quaternion)
  q v q^-1 = j
  rotated: (0, 1, 0)
  check: |v'| = |v| = 1, the real part is 0, and the rotation matrix gives the same vector
  rotation matrix:
    [0, -1, 0]
    [1, 0, 0]
    [0, 0, 1]
  (right-handed: counterclockwise looking down the axis towards the origin; -q gives the same rotation)
> quaternion product of i and j
  ucas: (i)*(j)
  note: i, j are quaternion units: ij = k = -ji
k
> is octonion multiplication associative
  ucas: algebra octonion associative
the octonions: dimension 8 over the reals, basis 1, e1, e2, e3, e4, e5, e6, e7
  associative: no: (e1 e2) e4 = e7 but e1 (e2 e4) = -e7
> are the quaternions commutative?
  ucas: algebra quaternion commutative
the quaternions: dimension 4 over the reals, basis 1, i, j, k
  commutative: no: i j = k but j i = -k
> the octonion multiplication table
  ucas: table octonion
multiplication table of the octonions (row times column), not symmetric: the product does not commute:
       |    1   e1   e2   e3   e4   e5   e6   e7
  -----+----------------------------------------
     1 |    1   e1   e2   e3   e4   e5   e6   e7
    e1 |   e1   -1   e3  -e2   e5  -e4  -e7   e6
    e2 |   e2  -e3   -1   e1   e6   e7  -e4  -e5
    e3 |   e3   e2  -e1   -1   e7  -e6   e5  -e4
    e4 |   e4  -e5  -e6  -e7   -1   e1   e2   e3
    e5 |   e5   e4  -e7   e6  -e1   -1  -e3   e2
    e6 |   e6   e7   e4  -e5  -e2   e3   -1  -e1
    e7 |   e7  -e6   e5   e4  -e3  -e2   e1   -1
  the Fano plane: the 7 lines (a, b, c) with e_a e_b = e_c, e_b e_c = e_a, e_c e_a = e_b:
    (1, 2, 3) (1, 4, 5) (1, 7, 6) (2, 4, 6) (2, 5, 7) (3, 4, 7) (3, 6, 5)
  every pair of units lies on one line; e_a^2 = -1 and e_a e_b = -e_b e_a for a != b
> zero divisors of the sedenions
  ucas: zerodivisors sedenion
zero divisors in the sedenions:
  (e1 - e10)(e4 + e15) = 0, although |e1 - e10| = |e4 + e15| = sqrt(2)
  so |ab| = |a||b| fails and the sedenions are not a division algebra (from dimension 16 on, every Cayley-Dickson algebra has them)
> the commutator of i and j
  ucas: commutator i, j
[a, b] = ab - ba = k - (-k) = 2k
  they do not commute
> domain colouring of (z^2-1)/(z^2+1) nofile
  ucas: plot (z^2-1)/(z^2+1) nofile
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
domain colouring of (z^2 - 1)/(z^2 + 1), z in [-2, 2] x [-2, 2]i
  hue = arg f (red: positive real, yellow-green: arg 2pi/3 side, cyan: negative real, blue-magenta: arg < 0); brightness rises from dark to light each time |f| doubles
  zeros (every hue meets, counterclockwise red, yellow, green, blue): -1 1
  poles (the hues in the opposite order): -i i
  image: 400x400, 46813 bytes
  (nofile: GIF checksum 0bb790e2)
> the limit of sin(z)/z as z approaches 0
  ucas: it(z) = sin(z)/z
  ucas: limit it z -> 0
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
limit of it as z -> 0:
  = 1
  checked numerically along 8 directions (arg(z - a) = 0, pi/4, ..., -pi/4), z -> a by factors of 4: all agree
  (it is undefined at 0 itself: a removable singularity)
